<phyphox version="1.14" locale="en">
  <title>Ultrasonic Leak Sweep</title>
  <category>Data centre</category>
  <icon>LEAK</icon>
  <description>
        WHAT IT DOES
        Hunts leaking compressed air and gas by the hiss you cannot hear.

        HOW
        Gas squeezing through a small hole goes turbulent - the air tumbles and churns instead of flowing smoothly. That churning makes noise across a huge range of pitches, including well above what people can hear. Almost everything ELSE in a plant room is low pitched. So near a leak, the share of high pitched noise jumps, even though the room does not get noticeably louder. That share is what this measures.

        HOW TO RUN IT
        1. Stand in clean air and press Set baseline here. The baseline is a first reading you compare everything else against.
        2. Walk the pipe run slowly, watching the number.
        3. Point the microphone end of the phone at joints, unions and valve stems - that is where leaks are.

        READ THIS BEFORE YOU RELY ON IT
        Real leak detectors listen at 25 to 40 kHz. kHz means thousands of times per second. NO phone can do that. A phone microphone physically cannot pick up anything above about 24 kHz, and it is already fading out below 22 kHz.

        So this listens at 15 to 22 kHz - only the very bottom edge of the sound a leak makes, where other noise still competes. It will find a decent leak in a quiet plant room. It will not find a small one, and it does not compete with a proper ultrasonic gun.

        Treat a positive reading as a reason to go and listen with your ear. Treat a negative reading as no information at all.

        AND FIRST, ASK WHETHER YOU NEED IT
        This tool exists for compressed air systems. If your site has no pneumatics, no compressor and no gas suppression pipework, there may be nothing here for it to find. Worth checking before you spend an afternoon with it.

        SAFETY
        Never put your hand near a suspected compressed air leak. A high pressure jet can push air or bits of dirt straight through your skin. Find it, mark it, report it.
    </description>

  <data-containers>
    <container size="16384">recording</container>
    <container>micRate</container>
    <container>micCnt</container>
    <container>micFrom</container>
    <container size="8192">block</container>
    <container>blockN</container>

    <container size="8192">winU</container>
    <container size="8192">win</container>
    <container size="8192">windowed</container>
    <container size="8192">re</container>
    <container size="8192">im</container>
    <container>nHalf</container>
    <container>nHalf1</container>
    <container>rateHalf</container>
    <container size="4097">freq</container>
    <container size="4096">halfF</container>
    <container size="4096">halfRe</container>
    <container size="4096">halfIm</container>
    <container size="4096">mag</container>
    <container size="4096">power</container>

    <container init="15000">fLow</container>
    <container init="22000">fHigh</container>
    <container>iLow</container>
    <container>iHigh</container>
    <container size="4096">bandPower</container>
    <container>sumBand</container>
    <container>sumTotal</container>

    <!-- spectral flatness of the band: 1 = broadband hiss (leak), 0 = a tone -->
    <container>countBand</container>
    <container>arithMean</container>
    <container size="4096">nP</container>
    <container size="4096">lnNP</container>
    <container>meanLnNP</container>
    <container>flatness</container>
    <container>ratio</container>
    <container>lnRatio</container>
    <container>ratioDb</container>

    <container init="0">baseline</container>
    <container init="0">hasBase</container>
    <container init="0">setBase</container>
    <container>excess</container>

    <container size="0">hT</container>
    <container size="0">hDb</container>
    <container>peakRaw</container>
    <container>peakExcess</container>
    <container>now</container>
  </data-containers>

  <input>
    <audio append="true">
      <output>recording</output>
      <output component="rate">micRate</output>
    </audio>
  </input>

  <views>
    <view label="Hunt">
      <value label="Above the clean-air baseline" unit="dB" size="3" precision="1">
        <input>excess</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Verdict">
        <input>excess</input>
        <map max="2">clean</map>
        <map max="5">slightly raised - keep sweeping</map>
        <map max="10">something is hissing near here</map>
        <map>strong - you are close to it</map>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Loudest point found so far" unit="dB" precision="1">
        <input>peakExcess</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Baseline set yet">
        <input>hasBase</input>
        <map max="0.5">NO - stand in clean air and set it</map>
        <map>yes</map>
      </value>
      <button label="Set baseline here">
        <input type="value">1</input>
        <output>setBase</output>
      </button>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <graph label="As you sweep" labelX="Time" unitX="s" labelY="High-frequency share" unitY="dB" partialUpdate="true">
        <input axis="x">hT</input>
        <input axis="y">hDb</input>
      </graph>
      <info label="Sweep slowly. The peak in this trace is where the leak is - walk back to it and narrow down by moving the phone a hand's width at a time."/>
    </view>

    <view label="Detail">
      <value label="High-frequency share of all sound" unit="dB" precision="2">
        <input>ratioDb</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Sound texture (1 = hiss, 0 = a pure tone)" size="2" precision="3">
        <input>flatness</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Is the high sound leak-like">
        <input>flatness</input>
        <map max="0.15">tonal - a whine or hum, most likely NOT a leak</map>
        <map max="0.35">mixed - part hiss, part tone</map>
        <map>broadband hiss - this is what a leak sounds like</map>
      </value>
      <info label="A leak is turbulent, so it hisses across a wide range of pitches at once. A power supply, an LED driver or a fan whine sits at ONE pitch. This texture score tells them apart, so a loud whine does not read as a leak. Read it together with the share above: a real find is BOTH raised share AND hiss-like texture. Give it a few seconds to settle - the score climbs as the reading averages out."/>
      <value label="Baseline" unit="dB" precision="2">
        <input>baseline</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Microphone sample rate" unit="Hz" precision="0">
        <input>micRate</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Highest frequency this phone can record" unit="Hz" precision="0">
        <input>rateHalf</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <graph label="Spectrum" logX="true" logY="true" labelX="Frequency" unitX="Hz" labelY="Level" unitY="a.u.">
        <input axis="x">halfF</input>
        <input axis="y">mag</input>
      </graph>
      <info label="A leak shows as the right-hand end of this curve lifting. If the curve falls off a cliff before 20 kHz, that is your microphone's filter, not the room going quiet."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <edit label="Band from" unit="Hz" default="15000" decimal="false">
        <output>fLow</output>
      </edit>
      <edit label="Band to" unit="Hz" default="22000" decimal="false">
        <output>fHigh</output>
      </edit>
    </view>

    <view label="Notes">
      <info label="WHY IT SHOWS A PROPORTION AND NOT A LOUDNESS"/>
      <info label="A plant room is loud, and it gets louder and quieter as you walk about. Almost all of that noise is low pitched."/>
      <info label="So instead of measuring how loud the high pitched hiss is, it measures what SHARE of the total the hiss makes up. Most of the room noise then cancels itself out, and walking closer to a fan no longer looks like a leak."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WHAT WILL FOOL IT"/>
      <info label="Anything else that hisses or whines up high. Air rushing through a grille. Coil whine from a power supply. Some LED drivers. Running water. And in a very quiet room, the phone's own microphone hiss."/>
      <info label="WIND is the worst. Air blowing across the microphone drowns out everything. In a hot aisle or standing in front of a fan, this reads nonsense - not a small error, complete nonsense."/>
      <info label="Confirm with your ear where you can. Most air leaks are audible close up once you know where to listen."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="THE LIMIT THAT MATTERS"/>
      <info label="Proper leak detectors listen at 25 to 40 thousand cycles per second, precisely because almost nothing else is up there to confuse them."/>
      <info label="A phone physically cannot go there. It hears sound in snapshots, 44 or 48 thousand a second. The rule is you can only catch a pitch up to half your snapshot rate - so about 22 to 24 thousand. Try to catch something faster and it looks slower than it really is, the way a fast wheel looks like it turns slowly backwards on film. The phone's own filter is already cutting in below that."/>
      <info label="So this works at 15 to 22 thousand: only the very bottom edge of the sound a leak makes, where other noise still lives. That is why a positive means go and listen, and a negative means nothing at all."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="ASK WHETHER YOU NEED THIS AT ALL"/>
      <info label="It exists for compressed air. If the site has no pneumatics, no compressor and no gas suppression pipework, there may be nothing for it to find. Cheaper to ask that question than to walk the site."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="SAFETY"/>
      <info label="Never put your hand near a suspected compressed air leak. A high pressure jet can push air or bits of dirt straight through your skin and cause a serious injury from a hole you can barely see. Locate it, mark it, report it."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WORDS THIS USES"/>
      <info label="baseline - a first reading you compare everything else against."/>
      <info label="kHz - thousands of times per second."/>
      <info label="turbulent - air tumbling and churning instead of flowing smoothly. It is what makes the hiss."/>
      <info label="ultrasonic - pitches too high for people to hear."/>
      <info label="a.u. - arbitrary units. The number only means something next to another number from the same test."/>
      <info label="the snapshot rule - a phone hears sound in snapshots and can only catch pitches up to half its snapshot rate. Faster ones look slower than they are, like a wheel on film."/>
    </view>
  </views>

  <analysis sleep="0.25">
    <timer>
      <output as="out" clear="false">now</output>
    </timer>

    <count>
      <input clear="false">recording</input>
      <output>micCnt</output>
    </count>
    <formula formula="max([1]-8192,0)">
      <input>micCnt</input>
      <output>micFrom</output>
    </formula>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" clear="false">micFrom</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">recording</input>
      <output>block</output>
    </subrange>
    <count>
      <input clear="false">block</input>
      <output clear="false">blockN</output>
    </count>

    <ramp>
      <input as="start" type="value">0</input>
      <input as="stop" type="value">1</input>
      <input as="length" clear="false">blockN</input>
      <output>winU</output>
    </ramp>
    <formula formula="0.5-0.5*cos(6.283185307179586*[1_])">
      <input>winU</input>
      <output>win</output>
    </formula>
    <multiply>
      <input as="factor">block</input>
      <input as="factor">win</input>
      <output>windowed</output>
    </multiply>
    <fft>
      <input>windowed</input>
      <output as="re">re</output>
      <output as="im">im</output>
    </fft>
    <divide>
      <input clear="false">micRate</input>
      <input type="value">2</input>
      <output clear="false">rateHalf</output>
    </divide>
    <divide>
      <input clear="false">blockN</input>
      <input type="value">2</input>
      <output clear="false">nHalf</output>
    </divide>
    <add>
      <input clear="false">nHalf</input>
      <input type="value">1</input>
      <output>nHalf1</output>
    </add>
    <ramp>
      <input as="start" type="value">0</input>
      <input as="stop" clear="false">rateHalf</input>
      <input as="length" clear="false">nHalf1</input>
      <output>freq</output>
    </ramp>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" type="value">1</input>
      <input as="to" clear="false">nHalf</input>
      <input as="in">freq</input>
      <input as="in">re</input>
      <input as="in">im</input>
      <output>halfF</output>
      <output>halfRe</output>
      <output>halfIm</output>
    </subrange>
    <formula formula="sqrt([1_]*[1_]+[2_]*[2_])">
      <input>halfRe</input>
      <input>halfIm</input>
      <output>mag</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="[1_]*[1_]">
      <input clear="false">mag</input>
      <output>power</output>
    </formula>

    <formula formula="max(round([1]*[2]/[3])-1,1)">
      <input clear="false">fLow</input>
      <input clear="false">blockN</input>
      <input clear="false">micRate</input>
      <output>iLow</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="min(max(round([1]*[2]/[3])-1,2),[4]-2)">
      <input clear="false">fHigh</input>
      <input clear="false">blockN</input>
      <input clear="false">micRate</input>
      <input clear="false">nHalf</input>
      <output>iHigh</output>
    </formula>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" clear="false">iLow</input>
      <input as="to" clear="false">iHigh</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">power</input>
      <output>bandPower</output>
    </subrange>
    <integrate>
      <input clear="false">bandPower</input>
      <output>sumBand</output>
    </integrate>
    <integrate>
      <input>power</input>
      <output>sumTotal</output>
    </integrate>
    <formula formula="([1]+0.000000000001)/([2]+0.000000000001)">
      <input clear="false">sumBand</input>
      <input>sumTotal</input>
      <output>ratio</output>
    </formula>
    <log>
      <input>ratio</input>
      <output>lnRatio</output>
    </log>
    <formula formula="4.342944819032518*[1]">
      <input>lnRatio</input>
      <output clear="false">ratioDb</output>
    </formula>

    <!-- Spectral flatness: geometric mean / arithmetic mean of the band power.
         A turbulent leak is broadband hiss (flat, near 1); a coil whine, LED
         driver or hum is tonal (peaky, near 0). Computed scale-free by first
         normalising each bin by the band's mean, then exp(mean(log)). One
         honest subtlety: a single spectrum of true broadband noise reads about
         0.56 (e^-gamma), not 1.0, because each bin is a noisy estimate - it
         climbs toward 1 the longer the reading settles. Still a 10x separation
         from a tone, which is all the discrimination needed. -->
    <count>
      <input clear="false">bandPower</input>
      <output>countBand</output>
    </count>
    <formula formula="[1]/max([2],1)">
      <input clear="false">sumBand</input>
      <input>countBand</input>
      <output>arithMean</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="[1_]/max([2],0.000000000000000001)">
      <input clear="false">bandPower</input>
      <input>arithMean</input>
      <output>nP</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="log([1_]+0.000000000001)">
      <input>nP</input>
      <output>lnNP</output>
    </formula>
    <average>
      <input as="buffer">lnNP</input>
      <output as="average">meanLnNP</output>
    </average>
    <formula formula="exp([1])">
      <input>meanLnNP</input>
      <output>flatness</output>
    </formula>

    <if equal="true">
      <input clear="false">setBase</input>
      <input type="value">1</input>
      <input clear="false">ratioDb</input>
      <output clear="false">baseline</output>
    </if>
    <if equal="true">
      <input clear="false">setBase</input>
      <input type="value">1</input>
      <input type="value">1</input>
      <output clear="false">hasBase</output>
    </if>
    <append>
      <input type="value">0</input>
      <output>setBase</output>
    </append>
    <subtract>
      <input clear="false">ratioDb</input>
      <input clear="false">baseline</input>
      <output clear="false">excess</output>
    </subtract>

    <append>
      <input clear="false">now</input>
      <output clear="false">hT</output>
    </append>
    <append>
      <input clear="false">ratioDb</input>
      <output clear="false">hDb</output>
    </append>
    <max>
      <input as="y" clear="false">hDb</input>
      <output as="max">peakRaw</output>
    </max>
    <subtract>
      <input>peakRaw</input>
      <input clear="false">baseline</input>
      <output>peakExcess</output>
    </subtract>
  </analysis>

  <export>
    <set name="Sweep">
      <data name="Time (s)">hT</data>
      <data name="High-frequency share (dB)">hDb</data>
    </set>
    <set name="Reading">
      <data name="High-frequency share (dB)">ratioDb</data>
      <data name="Baseline (dB)">baseline</data>
      <data name="Above baseline (dB)">excess</data>
      <data name="Microphone rate (Hz)">micRate</data>
    </set>
    <set name="Spectrum">
      <data name="Frequency (Hz)">halfF</data>
      <data name="Level (a.u.)">mag</data>
    </set>
  </export>
</phyphox>
